this helmet camera footage was taken  during a raid in uruzgan province in  afghanistan in 2012  and obtained by the four corners program  of australian broadcaster  abc the unarmed man is shot three times  at close range  killed by an australian special forces  soldier  a small number of people acted in an  aberrant way  that is impacted on families in  afghanistan  and elsewhere and they should be held  accountable  an independent inquiry investigated 55  incidents involving australian special  forces in afghanistan that allegedly  took place between 2005 and 2016  including accusations of unlawful  killings of prisoners  and civilians australians were deployed  alongside u.s and allied forces  following the 2001  september 11 attacks in new york the  inquiry began  in early 2016 and has been conducted  behind  closed doors since then it was  instigated by  a commander of special forces and  supported by the australian defense  force  but as it's progressed the alleged  incidents have become  a highly charged public debate  admiral chris barry a former chief of  the australian defense force  says it's important the public is  informed about the nature of the alleged  defenses  and any possible trials only in that way  will we be able to to show  people that we are serious about being a  force for good  and secondly when people don't do it as  we expect we're going to do something  about it  the international criminal court is also  investigating whether war crimes were  committed in afghanistan  by the taliban the afghan military and  u.s forces opposed by the u.s government  president donald trump has sanctioned  some icc staff i think we should be  encouraged by the process that australia  is undertaking here  but you know there's more that needs to  be done and we need to keep monitoring  and to ensure that that process  is actually effective and genuine more  than 26 000 australians served in  afghanistan over 13 years  the nation's longest military  involvement in any war  the question in australia is what impact  will the results of this inquiry have on  people's perceptions of the conflict  and those who took part nikola gage  al-jazeera  canberra joining me now is roger  shanahan who is a former australian army  officer and a research fellow at the  loewy institute for international policy  joins me now via skype from sydney  always good to have you on the program  mr shanahan i mean it's taken four years  to get this report  potentially uh to this stage where its  findings will be released on thursday  but surely the battle begins now from  all sides about prosecutions and defense  of what transpired in afghanistan  absolutely and this is one of the uh one  of those significant issues this  administrative inquiry has taken four  years  um the report has been eagerly awaited  but  really this is simply the start of the  next phase the public is going to be  informed about the findings of the  report  and then the legal processes take over  from here and that is going to take  several years to come yet  in your opinion um did the australian  armed forces serve impeccably and  impartially in afghanistan  you too served in afghanistan did you  ever hear  of these sorts of rumors or whispers  that  officers or uh those  on the front line acted unprofessionally  in any way  well australia had a had a policy  of uh doing inquiries as did a number of  other countries  into accusations or instances of  civilian casualties  i myself did several of those  so there was a pretty robust  procedure about investigating  accusations of civilian casualties  but the reality is that if you have  special forces who operate in  disaggregated groups  and incidents happen well away from  prying eyes then you can't  understand everything that goes on such  a complex battlefield so  overall certainly there was a robust  procedure for civilian casualties but  no process or procedure is going to  obviously encapsulate every incidence of  this 